/* ============================================================================
   limit-of-sinx-over-x.epher -- sin(x)/x at tiny x rounds to 1

   The classic squeeze-theorem limit sin(x)/x -> 1 as x -> 0 (Stewart,
   "Calculus", 8th ed., sec. 2.6) probed numerically at x = 1e-8, both
   signs. The ratio misses 1 by about x^2/6 = 1.7e-17 there, far below
   the calculator's precision, so it prints exactly 1; at x = 1e-4 the
   deficit x^2/6 = 1.7e-9 shows up in the last printed digits instead.

   The same limit defines the derivative of sin x at 0, and the builtin
   derivative (a numeric central difference, not symbolic) agrees.

   Accuracy: in exact arithmetic sin(x)/x at x = 1e-8 is 1 - 1.7e-25;
   every value below rounds at about the 12th printed digit.

   Run:  epher "epher scripts/calculus/limits/limit-of-sinx-over-x.epher"
   The knob to edit is `x` below.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob ---------------------------------------------------------------
const x = 1e-8

// ---- sin(x)/x at x = 1e-8 and -1e-8: both print the limit, 1 ----------------
sin(x)/x
sin(-x)/(-x)
abs(sin(x)/x - 1) < 1e-12

// ---- at x = 1e-4 and 1e-6 the x^2/6 deficit is (partly) visible -------------
for t in {1e-4, 1e-6} do sin(t)/t

// ---- cross-check: derivative of sin at 0, on a fresh variable t -----------
// (x is bound by the knob above, so a bound x would make the expression
// constant; t is free).
derivative(sin(t), 0)

/* ---- expected output ---------------------------------------------------------
= 0.00000001
= 1
= 1
= true
= {0.999999998333, 1}
= 1
*/
